course: dictionary
lesson: 14392
title: Ossified: hard as bone, and fixed in place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from science, and some come from history or argument. «Ossified» is one of them. In science, it means turned into bone. A baby's skeleton is partly soft cartilage, and that cartilage slowly becomes ossified as the child grows.
explain: Ossified also has a second meaning, used in a formal way. When ideas, rules or groups become fixed and stiff, and cannot change, we say they are ossified. «The club's rules were ossified, so nobody was allowed to suggest a new idea.» The rules are like old dry bones: hard, and not moving.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. In a bone sentence, try "turned into bone". In a rules sentence, try "stiff and unable to change". If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Ossify is the verb, and ossified is its past form, as in «The cartilage ossified».
fact: «Ossified» can describe a body part that has turned into bone, such as «the ossified skeleton of a grown-up».
fact: «Ossified» can also describe ideas, rules or groups that never change, such as «an ossified government».
fact: Common phrases are: became ossified, ossified ideas, and ossified rules.
fact: Ossified and fossilised sound alike, but a fossil is a very old remain of a living thing, while ossified means hardened into bone or made fixed.
exercise: «The soft cartilage in the young boy's leg had slowly become ossified.» What does ossified mean here? | Broken into pieces | Covered in soft skin | *Turned into bone | Stiff and unable to change
exercise: «Our school's ossified rules had not changed in fifty years.» What does ossified suggest? | *Fixed and unable to change | Very easy to follow | Brand new and shiny | Written in gold
exercise: Find and copy one word from this sentence that means the past form of ossify: «The cartilage ossified as the child grew.» | cartilage | child | grew | *ossified
test: «The old king would not listen, and his ossified court never changed a single law.» What does ossified mean? | Very loud and noisy | Full of light and glowing | Hard as bone and not made of any metal | *Fixed, stiff and unable to change
test: Which phrase describes a bone that has hardened from cartilage? | Ossified bone | Ossified rules | *Ossified skeleton | Ossified ideas
exam: Ayla says, «Ossified means very old, like a fossil.» Which answer corrects her best? | No, ossified means happy | Ossified means very soft and bendy | *Not quite: ossified means turned into bone or fixed and unable to change, while a fossil is an old remain of a living thing | Yes, ossified and fossil mean exactly the same
